sub:assertion {
<https://w3id.org/delphi/resource/claim/wolfram2002-ch09-06> a <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/Claim>;
    dc:description "Biological systems can show increasing order despite the Second Law mainly because they partition into parts (cells, organisms) separated by membrane-like boundaries, within which organized behavior can occur while the boundary survives.";
    dc:type "model-of-nature";
    <http://www.w3.org/2000/01/rdf-schema#label> "Biological order arises from partitioning into separated compartments";
    <https://w3id.org/delphi/ontology/context> "Stephen Wolfram, A New Kind of Science (Champaign, IL: Wolfram Media, 2002), Chapter 9, “Fundamental Physics”; section “Irreversibility and the Second Law of Thermodynamics” (from p. 441), p. 457. Scope: biological systems exhibiting self-organization"
}
